# Where is the best choice?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why is the best bundle where the curve just touches the budget line? What does the tangency mean in words?"
next: {"title":"When a price rises, why do you buy less — for two reasons?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/76-when-a-price-rises-why-do-you-buy-less.cast"}

## Three weeks, same price
use: indifference_budget
with:
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    income: 60
    price_x: 6
    price_y: 3
    preference: balanced
    curves: 2
    show_optimum: false
    x_good: Fish suppers
    y_good: Coffees
    x_max: 12
    y_max: 24
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 115
      w: 720
      h: 565

    dot pA #3d3833 at.data [1,18] hidden true
    dot pB #3d3833 at.data [5,10] hidden true
    dot pC #3d3833 at.data [9,2] hidden true
    scratch count work ["A: 1+18 = 19","B: 5+10 = 15","C: 9+2 = 11"] x 845 y 500 font_size 22 hidden true
    scratch price work ["Budget $60","Supper $6","Coffee $3"] x 845 y 500 font_size 22 hidden true
    text cue_touch "just touches" #b5482e at.data [7.4,11.2] font_size 24 hidden true
    text lA "A" at.data [1.9,19.6] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lB "B" at.data [5.7,11.4] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lC "C" at.data [9.6,3.8] font_size 26 hidden true

    card title "Where is the best choice?"

You have sixty dollars a week for two treats. Of all the mixes you can afford, where is the best one?
    draw axes

B: Take Nora, an apprentice at the Harbourtown boatyard. (@draw price_box price_line_1 price_line_2 price_line_3@) A fish supper costs six dollars, a coffee three. The numbers are illustrative.

Every week she can afford sits  (@draw budget_line@)on this budget line: ten suppers, or twenty coffees, or any mix between.

    erase price

Three weeks, each costing exactly sixty. A: (@draw pA lA@) one supper and eighteen coffees. B (@draw pB lB@): five suppers and ten coffees. C: n (@draw pC lC@)ine suppers and two coffees.

B: My guess is A. Ninete (@draw count_box count_line_1 count_line_2 count_line_3@)en treats beats fifteen or eleven. More is more.

    ask "If you were Nora, which week would you pick? Tap A, B or C." choose ["pA","pB","pC"] judge false
        right: Noted. Now let's see what Nora would pick.

Your pick tells us your taste. Nora's taste is  (@draw ic_1@)drawn as indifference curves, each one joining weeks she likes equally.

And A and C sit on the same curve. To her, the coffee week and the supper week are just as good.
    highlight pA pC effect circle

    erase count

B: So counting treats gets it wrong. What about B?
    highlight pB effect pulse

B li (@draw ic_2@)es on a higher curve. It beats both.

And look how that curve meets the line. It doesn't cross it; i (@draw cue_touch@)t just touches, right at B.

B: Coincidence? Or is the best week always where a curve just touches the line?
    highlight pB ic_2

    pause 0.5

## Why the touching point
use: indifference_budget
with:
    labels: false
    axis_numbers: true
    income: 60
    price_x: 6
    price_y: 3
    preference: balanced
    curves: 3
    show_optimum: true
    x_good: Fish suppers
    y_good: Coffees
    x_max: 12
    y_max: 24
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 115
      w: 720
      h: 565

    dot pA #3d3833 at.data [1,18] hidden true
    dot pC #3d3833 at.data [9,2] hidden true
    text lA "A" at.data [1.9,19.6] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lB "B" at.data [5.7,11.4] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lC "C" at.data [8.3,0.9] font_size 26 hidden true
    path seg #f2c14e points [[1,18],[9,2]] data true style.opacity 0.7 style.stroke_width 10 hidden true
    path ic32 smooth thick #87a878 points [[1.4,22.86],[1.6,20],[1.8,17.78],[2,16],[2.3,13.91],[2.6,12.31],[3,10.67],[3.5,9.14],[4,8],[5,6.4],[6,5.33],[7,4.57],[8,4],[9,3.56],[10,3.2],[11,2.91],[11.6,2.76]] data true hidden true
    dot nora #b5482e at.data [1,18] hidden true
    dot nora2 #b5482e at.data [9,2] hidden true
    text cue_better "better, same $60" #b5482e at.data [5.2,5.4] font_size 24 hidden true
    text cue_reach "out of reach" #8f887c at.data [9.3,16] font_size 24 hidden true

    card title "Why the touching point?"

B: Best and touching, at the same spot. Why should those go together?
    draw axes budget_line ic_1 ic_2 pA optimum pC lA lB lC

Start at  (@draw nora@)A. Its curve cuts through the line, so part (@draw seg@) of the line runs above that curve.

And above the curve is better. So from A there  (@draw cue_better@)are weeks she'd like more and can still afford.

Swap two coffe (@move nora to.data [2,16] duration 1.2@)es for one supper. Still sixty dollars, and s (@erase cue_better@)he's (@draw ic32@) already on a higher curve.

Keep swappi (@move nora to.data [3,14] duration 0.9@)ng. Three suppers,  (@move nora to.data [4,12] duration 0.9@)four: every step climbs.

B: Unti (@move nora to.data [5,10] duration 1@)l B. One more swap, six suppers and eig (@move nora to.data [6,8] duration 1@)ht coffees, and she slips back d (@move nora to.data [5,10] duration 0.8@)own.

B: From C it (@draw nora2@)'s the same walk, the other way (@move nora2 to.data [8,4] duration 1@): give up suppers for  (@move nora2 to.data [5,10] duration 1.6@)coffees and climb toward B.

    erase seg ic32 nora2 nora

So wherever a curve crosses the line, there's a better week along it. Only where a curve just touches is there nowhere higher to go.
    highlight ic_2 optimum

Higher cur (@draw ic_3@)ves exist, of course. This one never reaches th (@draw cue_reach@)e line: out of reach on sixty dollars.

    erase cue_reach pA pC lA lB lC

    draw guide_x guide_y

Try it: drag the end of the line to make suppers cheaper or dearer, and watch where the touching point goes.
    explore

B: Wherever the line goes, the best week sits where a curve just touches it.
    highlight optimum ic_2

    pause 0.5

## What the touching means
