sources:
    - id: gossen1854
      title: Entwickelung der Gesetze des menschlichen Verkehrs, und der daraus fließenden Regeln für menschliches Handeln
      authors: Hermann Heinrich Gossen
      year: 1854
      finding: States the rule that a budget is best spent when the last unit of money brings equal enjoyment in every use (Gossen's second law); the book was ignored until Adamson and Jevons found it in 1878 (Jevons, Theory of Political Economy, 2nd ed., 1879).

    dot pA #3d3833 at.data [1,18] hidden true
    dot pC #3d3833 at.data [9,2] hidden true
    text lA "A" at.data [1.9,19.6] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lB "B" at.data [5.7,11.4] font_size 26 hidden true
    text lC "C" at.data [9.6,3.8] font_size 26 hidden true
    path tA #b5482e points [[1,18],[2,9]] data true style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    path tC #b5482e points [[6.8,2.49],[11.2,1.51]] data true style.stroke_width 5 hidden true
    path tB #b5482e points [[3,14],[7,6]] data true style.stroke_width 6 hidden true
    scratch deal work ["Price: 2 coffees","At A: up to 9","At C: just ¼","At B: 2 = 2"] x 850 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    math rule cites ["gossen1854"] tex "\\text{MRS} = \\frac{P_{\\text{supper}}}{P_{\\text{coffee}}}" size 34 x 850 y 250 hidden true
    text gossen "Gossen, 1854" #8a5fa8 x 850 y 160 font_size 26 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch m work ["U = √(x·y)  →  MRS = y / x",{"tex":"\\frac{y}{x} = \\frac{6}{3} = 2"},"6x + 3y = 60","x = 5,  y = 10"] x 520 y 560 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "What does the touching mean?"

    hide guide_x guide_y

Touching means the curve and the line have the same slope there. And each slope is a price.
    draw axes budget_line ic_1 ic_2 pA optimum pC lA lB lC

The line's slope  (@draw deal_box deal_line_1@)is the shop's deal: one supper cos (@highlight budget_line@)ts two coffees, anywhere along it.

B: The curve's slope is Nora's own deal: how many coffees she'd give for one more supper. Economists call it the marginal rate of substitution.
    highlight ic_1

At A her curve is (@draw tA@) steep. For a second supper, she' (@draw deal_line_2@)d give up as many as nine coffees.

B: And the shop only asks two. A bargain she should take, again and again.
    highlight deal_line_1 deal_line_2

    quiz "Nora is at C: nine suppers, two coffees. Should she buy more suppers, or fewer?" voice b
        right: Fewer. At C a supper is worth almost nothing to her in coffees, and the shop pays two for each one she gives up.
        wrong: Her price at C against the shop's: here, fewer suppers.
        * More suppers
        + Fewer suppers, more coffees
        * Stay put

At C it's the revers (@draw tC@)e. She'd let a supper go for a quarter  (@draw deal_line_3@)of a coffee, and the shop gives her two.

    erase tA tC

At B the two dea (@draw tB@)ls agree: a supper is worth two coffees to her, and  (@draw deal_line_4@)it costs two coffees. No swap helps.

B: So in words: keep swapping  (@draw rule@)until what the last supper is worth to you equals what it costs.

In 1854 a Prussian  (@draw gossen@)civil servant, Hermann Gossen, made it a law: spend so the last coin brings the same pleasure everywhere.

His book flopped, and was found again onl (@erase gossen@)y in 1878.

B: And here's the quiet consequence. Everyone who shops at these prices ends up valuing the last supper at two coffees.
    highlight deal_line_4

So no swap between any two of them would help either. The prices line everybody up, without anyone ever meeting.
    highlight rule effect box

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own
        intro: There's a short calculation behind B.

@math

    erase mathq

Here it is. Say Nora's taste is th (@draw m_box m_line_1@)e square root of suppers times coffees. Then her rate is coffees over suppers.

B: Set it equal to the price ra (@draw m_line_2@)tio, six over three: she wants twice as many coffees as suppers.

Put that into the budget, six (@draw m_line_3 m_line_4@) x plus three y is sixty, and out come five suppers and ten coffees.

    erase m

@test

    erase mathq

    pause 0.3

## Corners, and does anyone do this?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Nora would let you do her shopping.
    good: Your curves and the shop's prices mostly agree.
    poor: 'Compare the two prices: yours and the shop''s.'
    none: The shop is still open. Try again.
