sources:
    - id: fmi
      title: Supermarket Facts
      authors: FMI – The Food Industry Association
      year: 2025
      finding: The average US supermarket carries 33,248 items (2025).
      url: https://www.fmi.org/our-research/supermarket-facts
    - id: chen2006
      title: How basic are behavioral biases? Evidence from capuchin monkey trading behavior
      authors: Chen, Lakshminarayanan, Santos
      year: 2006
      finding: Capuchins trading tokens for food responded to price and wealth changes consistently with GARP, and also showed reference dependence and loss aversion.
    - id: choi2014
      title: Who is (more) rational?
      authors: Choi, Kariv, Müller, Silverman
      year: 2014
      finding: In a large Dutch household panel (CentERpanel), consistency with utility maximisation varied widely; more consistent subjects had higher income and education and 15-19% more wealth per SD of consistency.

    text lB "B" at.data [5.7,11.4] font_size 26 hidden true
    path yours smooth #8a5fa8 points [[0,20],[1,19.48],[2,18.92],[3,18.32],[4,17.68],[5,17],[6,16.28],[7,15.52],[8,14.72],[9,13.88],[10,13],[11,12.08]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text lyours "yours" #8a5fa8 at.data [10.6,14.2] font_size 24 hidden true
    dot corner #b5482e at.data [0,20] hidden true
    text lcorner "corner" #b5482e at.data [1.7,21.6] font_size 26 hidden true
    text items "Supermarket:\n~33,000 items" cites ["fmi"] x 860 y 470 font_size 30 hidden true
    text ev2 "Capuchins, 2006" cites ["chen2006"] x 640 y 520 font_size 40 hidden true
    text ev3 "Dutch panel, 2014" cites ["choi2014"] x 640 y 420 font_size 40 hidden true
    text sure1 "Respond to prices: mostly agreed" x 500 y 290 font_size 40 hidden true
    text sure2 "Really optimise: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 215 font_size 40 hidden true
    icon bike size 170 set twemoji x 170 y 440 icon_look picture of bicycle hidden true
    icon ev2i size 90 set twemoji x 400 y 520 icon_look picture of monkey hidden true
    icon ev3i size 90 set twemoji x 400 y 420 icon_look picture of "people holding hands" hidden true

    card title "Corners, and does anyone do this?"

    hide guide_x guide_y ic_1

B: Lovely. But I never buy fish suppers. Where's my touching point?
    draw axes budget_line ic_2 optimum lB

    fade ic_2 optimum lB to 0.3

Nowhere ins (@draw yours lyours@)ide. You sit in the corner: for  (@draw corner lcorner@)you, even the first supper isn't worth the two coffees it costs.

B: And corners are everywhere. A supermarket carries ab (@draw items@)out thirty-three thousand items, and we leave nearly all of them on the shelf.

    erase items

So at a corner the rule becomes: what a supper is worth to you is less than its price.
    highlight corner effect circle

Second objection, though: nobody draws curves at the checkout.
    hide axes budget_line ic_2 optimum lB yours lyours corner lcorner

    draw bike

    pause 0.3

B: Nobody has to. The claim is only that people choose as if they did, the way a cyclist balances without knowing any physics.
    highlight bike effect pulse

B: And as-if goes a long way. In 2006, capuchin monkeys tradi (@draw ev2i ev2@)ng tokens for food bought more of whatever got cheaper. They obeyed price theory, and they also hated losses.

And people? A 2014 st (@draw ev3i ev3@)udy of a large Dutch panel found most choices close to consistent, far from perfect, and the more consistent were richer.

How sure are we? That people respond to prices th (@draw sure1@)e way the touching point predicts: mostly agreed.

That anyone  (@draw sure2@)really optimises: contested.

    erase bike ev2 ev3 sure1 sure2 ev2i ev3i

    show axes budget_line ic_2 optimum

    fade ic_2 optimum to 1

    quiz "Ole has 4 pizzas and 6 beers. A pizza costs 3 beers, but he'd give only 1 beer for one more pizza. What should he do?" intro "Three new cases."
        right: Less pizza, more beer. A pizza is worth one beer to him but costs three, so each pizza he gives up buys three beers he values more.
        wrong: Weigh a pizza's worth to Ole against its price: here, less pizza.
        * Buy more pizza
        + Buy less pizza, more beer
        * Stay put

    quiz "Siv never takes a taxi. A ride costs as much as 10 bus rides, and her first taxi ride would be worth 4 bus rides to her. Is never taking taxis a mistake?" voice b
        right: No mistake. Her first taxi is worth 4 bus rides but costs 10, so her best choice has no taxis at all: a corner.
        wrong: Weigh her first ride's worth against its price: here, no mistake.
        + No: it's her best choice
        * Yes: she should take a few
        * Can't tell without her income

    quiz "A café raises cake from 2 coffees to 4. Ivar's parents top up his allowance so his usual order still just fits. At that order, he'd give 3 coffees for one more cake. What should he do?"
        right: Fewer cakes, more coffees. His usual order still fits, but a cake now costs 4 coffees and is worth only 3 to him, so each cake he gives up buys coffees he values more.
        wrong: Weigh a cake's worth to Ivar against its new price: here, fewer cakes.
        * Buy more cakes
        * Keep his usual order
        + Buy fewer cakes, more coffees

So where is the best choice? Where no swap helps any more: what the next supper is worth to you equals what it costs.
    highlight optimum ic_2

    hide lB

And when a price changes, the touching point moves, which is where demand comes from.
    animate price_x 8 duration 2.5 ghost true

    pause 0.5

## Where next
